      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kannapolis began as a textile town when Cannon Mills opened its first factory in 1908 along the historic wagon road between Salisbury and Charlotte.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kannapolis%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name shifted from Cannon Mills to Kannapolis when founder J.W. Cannon requested a “K” to honor the town’s roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kannapolis%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Gem Theatre, an art deco landmark opened in 1936 and rebuilt in 1948, now graces the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorecabarrus.com/cities-towns/kannapolis/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kannapolis hosts the North Carolina Research Campus, a public‑private partnership that focuses on food, nutrition, and biotech research.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kannapolis%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The city celebrates motorsports heritage at the Curb Museum, which houses the NC Music Hall of Fame and a racing exhibit featuring the Earnhardt family.",
